{:edb/db-before-t 481 :edb/db-after-t 482 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533794 50 #inst "2026-10-05T00:24:28.480Z" 13194139533794 true] [17592186064228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251576 13194139533794 true] [17592186064228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251583 13194139533794 true] [17592186064228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251590 13194139533794 true] [17592186064228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251597 13194139533794 true] [17592186064229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251555 13194139533794 true] [17592186064229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251562 13194139533794 true] [17592186064229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251622 13194139533794 true] [17592186064229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251629 13194139533794 true] [17592186064230 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251569 13194139533794 true] [17592186064230 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251604 13194139533794 true] [17592186064230 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251608 13194139533794 true] [17592186064230 1020 #edb/ref 17592186251615 13194139533794 true] [17592186064427 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533794 true] [17592186064427 1145 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(1)=2-(1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(2x-1) \\\\  & =2(1)-1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(2-x) \\\\  & =2-(1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=1=f(1).$  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\"" 13194139533794 true] [17592186064669 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533794 true] [17592186064669 1145 "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ exists and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)=f(-2)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)}({(f(x))}^{2}+4) & =({2}^{2}+4)=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533794 true] [17592186064723 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533794 true] [17592186064723 1145 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $f(3)=0,$ so $f(3)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and find that they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2} \\\\  & =9, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(2x+3) \\\\  & =2(3)+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9.\\,\\,✓$ $\\,\\,\\,$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)=9,$ while $f(3)=0.$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=3$ because ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)\\ne f(3).$" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251555 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251556 13194139533794 true] [17592186251555 1089 "q-104130" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251555 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533794 true] [17592186251555 1100 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-2?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}, & x<-2 \\\\ 4, & x=-2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}, & x>-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251555 1113 #uuid "aae228c9-7097-5c88-acad-0bde9e65eba0" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251555 1145 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- $f(-2)=4,$ so $f(-2)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{2} \\\\  & =4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the one-sided limits exist but are not equal. $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-2$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist." 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251557 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251558 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251559 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251560 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251561 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1041 "selection" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251559 13194139533794 true] [17592186251556 1110 #uuid "c7b7a241-5fdb-5f5f-9b26-60ba337591a1" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251557 1034 "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251557 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251557 1111 #uuid "4828487c-e173-576a-b8ae-c55d54b7cabd" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251558 1034 "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)$ does not exist" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251558 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251558 1111 #uuid "949206f3-36c5-5bd7-900f-3f917a863e8a" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251559 1034 "${lim}_{x\\to (-2)}f(x)$ does not exist" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251559 1111 #uuid "52b53ca9-f0e8-5cf7-be5e-