of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)}f(x)=f(-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251583 1113 #uuid "ca3f1027-fa37-5b41-836a-b53bef56e3f1" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251583 1145 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the first branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(-1)=\\sqrt{3-(-1)}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is false. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\sqrt{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-(-1)} \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(-1)+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement III is false since the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=-1$ does not exist. $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251585 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251586 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251587 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251588 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251589 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1041 "selection" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251586 13194139533794 true] [17592186251584 1110 #uuid "c89d7f1e-f33c-53c7-b62c-1fa840e86298" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251585 1034 "I and IV only" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251585 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251585 1111 #uuid "71f0083b-da14-55ab-8683-9d1e2f675e01" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251586 1034 "I only" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251586 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251586 1111 #uuid "969c0fdb-6009-571c-bc7c-ff6b76f9be86" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251587 1034 "I and II only" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251587 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251587 1111 #uuid "e9575c37-fc5a-56f8-a3de-9f9f695dc702" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251588 1034 "I, II, and III only" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251588 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251588 1111 #uuid "3880a286-8228-5cfd-b329-e68bf6ce00f2" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251589 1034 "II only" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251589 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251589 1111 #uuid "f3a76e25-b0bc-540a-ac1b-bf188ebde065" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251590 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251591 13194139533794 true] [17592186251590 1089 "q-152926" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251590 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533794 true] [17592186251590 1100 "Consider the following function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x, & x<0 \\\\ 1, & x=0 \\\\ 3x, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding $f(x)?$\n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=f(0)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251590 1113 #uuid "9fb59805-3754-53e9-acab-d2034fbd41f2" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251590 1145 "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x) \\\\  & =2(0) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(3x) \\\\  & =3(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, according to the continuity test, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251592 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251593 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251594 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251595 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251596 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1041 "selection" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1104 #edb/ref 17592186251594 13194139533794 true] [17592186251591 1110 #uuid "03c42f49-eca4-548e-ba2a-946523e99270" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251592 1034 "I only" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251592 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 true] [17592186251592 1111 #uuid "cbd22ea0-a827-5c8a-806c-db2f6166d818" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251593 1034 "I, II, and IV only" 13194139533794 true] [17592186251593 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533794 t